{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>First occurrence</b><br>Like Carmel. Color [i.e., its hue; and they say it is the second meaning] — and in Chronicles, כרמיל and בוץ." "דלת — in the language of Kedar, the lock of hair flowing over the cheek [from דלל]." "ברהטים — like (ברהטים) in the water channels." "<b>Second occurrence</b><br>Like Carmel — like the color [of Carmel]." "Like argaman — for it is soft to the touch." "A king bound ברהטים. And every king would desire to be bound and tied with the cords of her hair [this matter was mentioned by the chieftains of Kedar who long, as in 'How fair and how pleasant you are' — there is no delight in the world for the soul, nor anything fair and pleasant, like longing.] And some say the meaning of ברהטים is that the hair resembles [one beneath another, like the appearance of water] flowing in channels. And some say the king is the head." "And after he likened her to a palm tree, he longed to be with her." "<b>Third occurrence</b><br>Your head — [this is King Messiah, who is of the sons of David.]" "[And your flowing locks.] — Messiah son of Joseph." "A king bound — a hint that he is Messiah son of David, bound until the end arrives, for on the day Jerusalem was destroyed he was born."], :book :song-of-songs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {9 "The parenthetical (נ) in 'נחרבה' appears to mark a corrected/inserted letter in the manuscript; translated as if reading נחרבה 'was destroyed.'"}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra's commentary on the Canticles" "http://beta.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH002030344/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:44:59.787027167Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}